= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The channel acts only on the input system $Q$; the reference $R$ is untouched. Write
$$
\omega_{RB}=(\operatorname{id}_R\otimes\Phi)(|\Psi^\rho\rangle\langle\Psi^\rho|_{RQ}).
$$
The <coherent information> is
$$
\boxed{I_c(\Phi,\rho)=S(B)_\omega-S(RB)_\omega=-S(R\mid B)_\omega}.
$$
The reference marginal remains $\rho_R$, whose nonzero eigenvalues agree with those of $\rho$. Unlike a classical information quantity, coherent information can be negative.

\Image[paper-48-channel.png]
{title=Quantum channel acting on one half of a purification, with untouched reference and discarded environment}

The lower diagram uses a <Stinespring dilation>: append an environment in a fixed <pure state>, apply a joint <unitary operator>, and discard the environment by <partial trace>. Before discarding it, the global $RBE$ state is pure.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Implement the <quantum channel> by a unitary dilation with a pure initial environment. The final state on $RBE$ is pure. The <Schmidt decomposition> across either bipartition gives equal complementary <Von Neumann entropies>, so $S(RB)=S(E)$ and $S(B)=S(RE)$. By the <subadditivity of Von Neumann entropy>,
$$
I_c(\Phi,\rho)=S(RE)-S(E)\leq S(R).
$$
The reference is untouched and purifies the input, hence $S(R)=S(\rho)$. Therefore **$\boxed{I_c(\Phi,\rho)\leq S(\rho)}$.**

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

After the first <quantum channel>, denote the joint reference-output state by $\omega_{RB_1}$. Dilate the second <CPTP map> as an <linear isometry> $B_1\to B_2E_2$, with the resulting state denoted $\tau$. <Linear isometries> preserve nonzero eigenvalues, both globally and on the transformed output, giving
$$
I_c(\Phi_1,\rho)=S(B_2E_2)_\tau-S(RB_2E_2)_\tau.
$$
Tracing out $E_2$ implements the second channel, so
$$
I_c(\Phi_2\circ\Phi_1,\rho)=S(B_2)_\tau-S(RB_2)_\tau.
$$
The <strong subadditivity of Von Neumann entropy> for $R,B_2,E_2$ gives $S(RB_2)+S(B_2E_2)\geq S(B_2)+S(RB_2E_2)$. Rearranging proves the <data-processing inequality for coherent information>:
$$
\boxed{I_c(\Phi_1,\rho)\geq I_c(\Phi_2\circ\Phi_1,\rho)}.
$$

